# C# File Handling Operations & Dialog Controls (Week 5 / Chapter 4)

## 📌 Project Overview
This repository contains practical C# Windows Forms implementations focusing on **File Handling** and **Dialog Controls**, based on Chapter 4 of *Starting Out with Visual C#* by Tony Gaddis. 

The project demonstrates how to safely write data without overwriting history, read files line-by-line using streaming loops, and use native Windows dialog boxes (`OpenFileDialog`) to select and load files dynamically.

---

## 🛠️ File Handling Examples & Implementation Guide

### Example 1: Safe File Writing (`StreamWriter` & `AppendText`)
* **How it works:** This function creates or opens a text file using `File.AppendText()`. Unlike `CreateText()` which erases existing data, `AppendText` preserves prior history and adds new entries on a fresh line.
* **Code Implementation:**
  ```csharp
  private void btnWrite_Click(object sender, EventArgs e)
  {
      try
      {
          // Opens the file in append mode to preserve old records
          StreamWriter outputFile = File.AppendText("students.txt");

          outputFile.WriteLine("Fathi Isse Mohamed");
          outputFile.WriteLine("Computer Science Student");
          
          outputFile.Close();

          MessageBox.Show("Xogta si guul leh ayaa loo qoray!", "Success", MessageBoxButtons.OK, MessageBoxIcon.Information);
      }
      catch (Exception ex)
      {
          MessageBox.Show("Khalad ayaa dhacay: " + ex.Message, "Error", MessageBoxButtons.OK, MessageBoxIcon.Error);
      }
  }


  example 2: Line-by-Line File Reading (StreamReader & EndOfStream)
How it works: This function uses a StreamReader combined with an EndOfStream loop (while (!inputFile.EndOfStream)) to read text data line-by-line and populate a ListBox dynamically[cite: 1].

Code Implementation:

C#
private void btnRead_Click(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
    try
    {
        lstOutput.Items.Clear();

        if (File.Exists("students.txt"))
        {
            StreamReader inputFile = File.OpenText("students.txt");

            // Loop through the stream until the end of the file is reached
            while (!inputFile.EndOfStream)
            {
                string line = inputFile.ReadLine();
                lstOutput.Items.Add(line);
            }

            inputFile.Close();
        }
        else
        {
            MessageBox.Show("Faylku ma jiro!", "Warning", MessageBoxButtons.OK, MessageBoxIcon.Warning);
        }
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        MessageBox.Show("Khalad ayaa dhacay: " + ex.Message, "Error", MessageBoxButtons.OK, MessageBoxIcon.Error);
    }
}

Example 3: Graphical File Selection (OpenFileDialog Control)
How it works: Utilizes the invisible OpenFileDialog control to display standard Windows file browsing boxes. It checks if the user clicked DialogResult.OK, retrieves the file path via .Filename, and reads its contents[cite: 1].

Code Implementation:

C#
private void btnOpenFile_Click(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
    try
    {
        OpenFileDialog openFileDialog1 = new OpenFileDialog();
        openFileDialog1.InitialDirectory = @"C:\";
        openFileDialog1.Filter = "Text Files (*.txt)|*.txt|All Files (*.*)|*.*";

        if (openFileDialog1.ShowDialog() == DialogResult.OK)
        {
            StreamReader reader = File.OpenText(openFileDialog1.FileName);
            lstOutput.Items.Clear();

            while (!reader.EndOfStream)
            {
                string line = reader.ReadLine();
                lstOutput.Items.Add(line);
            }

            reader.Close();
        }
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        MessageBox.Show("Khalad ayaa dhacay: " + ex.Message, "Error", MessageBoxButtons.OK, MessageBoxIcon.Error);
    }
}

